ffice of the President","years":[1995]},{"country_code":"US","id":"I4210115145","name":"University of Minnesota, Duluth","years":[1992]},{"country_code":"IN","id":"I40912709","name":"Institute of Physics, Bhubaneshwar","years":[1990]},{"country_code":"US","id":"I97018004","name":"Stanford University","years":[1983,1984,1985]}],"author_id":"A5067839341","ca_plastic_works_count":2,"ca_works_count":2,"citations_by_year":[{"all":2029,"plastic":0,"year":2017},{"all":2035,"plastic":0,"year":2018},{"all":2211,"plastic":0,"year":2019},{"all":2289,"plastic":0,"year":2020},{"all":2301,"plastic":0,"year":2021},{"all":2281,"plastic":3,"year":2022},{"all":2180,"plastic":13,"year":2023},{"all":2135,"plastic":15,"year":2024},{"all":2079,"plastic":32,"year":2025},{"all":1640,"plastic":25,"year":2026}],"cited_by_count":51972,"domain_tags":[],"early_career":false,"first_pub_year":1983,"first_year":2022,"h_index":94,"institution_ids":["I154570441"],"last_year":2023,"m_quotient":2.19,"name":"Glenn H. Fredrickson","orcid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6716-9017","plastic_basis":"openalex","plastic_citations":88,"plastic_works_count":2,"primary_affiliations":[{"country_code":"US","id":"I154570441","name":"University of California, Santa Barbara","source":"openalex_last_known"},{"country_code":"US","id":"I2803209242","name":"University of California System","source":"openalex_last_known"}],"rising_pct":76,"rising_score":0.0072,"work_count":2,"work_ids":["W4286615442","W4375862850"]},"schema_version":"s3.1","works":[{"abstract":"formation of block or graft copolymers during blending operations. Typically, such reactive blending exploits reversible or irreversible covalent or hydrogen bonds to produce the desired copolymer, but there are other options. Here, we argue that ionic bonds and electrostatic correlations represent an underutilized tool for polymer compatibilization and in tailoring materials for applications ranging from sustainable polymer alloys to organic electronics and solid polymer electrolytes. The theoretical basis for ionic compatibilization is surveyed and placed in the context of existing experimental literature and emerging classes of functional polymer materials. We conclude with a perspective on how electrostatic interactions might be exploited in plastic waste upcycling.","author_count":10,"author_ids":["A5001305555","A5027036200","A5034985589","A5046560705","A5056892634","A5057882312","A5065253425","A5066596999","A5067839341","A5082693255"],"author_institution_ids":["I154570441"],"authors":[{"id":"A5067839341","name":"Glenn H. Fredrickson"},{"id":"A5066596999","name":"Shuyi Xie"},{"id":"A5065253425","name":"Jerrick Edmund"},{"id":"A5082693255","name":"My Linh Le"},{"id":"A5056892634","name":"Dan Sun"},{"id":"A5057882312","name":"Douglas J. Grzetic"},{"id":"A5027036200","name":"Daniel L. Vigil"},{"id":"A5034985589","name":"Kris T. Delaney"},{"id":"A5046560705","name":"Michael L. Chabinyc"},{"id":"A5001305555","name":"Rachel A. Segalman"}],"ca_any_author":true,"ca_led":true,"cited_by_count":56,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acspolymersau.2c00026","domain_tags":["analytical_methods"],"facet_tags":[],"institution_ids":["I154570441"],"is_oa":true,"journal":"ACS Polymers Au","last_updated":"2026-11-11T12:16:26.678958","license":"cc-by","needs_review":false,"oa_status":"gold","oa_url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acspolymersau.2c00026","openalex_topics":["T10389","T10405","T11471"],"plastic_related":true,"plastic_tier":"core","primary_topic":"T10389","pub_date":"2022-07-22","study_type":"review","title":"Ionic Compatibilization of Polymers","type":"review","work_id":"W4286615442","year":2022},{"abstract":"Chemically dissimilar polymers are rarely miscible due to a specific entropy of mixing that scales as 1/ N, where N is the degree of polymerization. As a consequence, the compatibilization of immiscible polymer blends presents a major challenge to plastics recycling efforts. We demonstrate that when the chain ends of two highly immiscible polymers are functionalized with reacting acid and base groups, ionic junctions form in situ by proton transfer, leading to the stabilization of interfaces and the suppression of macroscopic phase separation. Due to the dynamic nature of proton transfer, the microstructure can be tuned via ionic bond energy ( h ) and segregation strength (\u03c7 N ). In this work, a library of ionically end-functionalized polystyrene (PS) and polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) is used to demonstrate the importance of end-group acidity/basicity and molecular weight on compatibilization. Well-ordered lamellae result from blending polymers with strong acid and base units, while less ordered, more swollen microphases occur in systems with weak acid and base end groups or larger molar mass. Heating the ionic blends results in reverse proton transfer and destruction of the close-contact ion pairs. As a result, domains are swollen with the resulting unbonded homopolymers, which in sufficient amounts, leads to macrophase separation. Moreover, external salt ions screen the electrostatic attractions and disturb ionic compatibilization. The self-consistent field theory (SCFT) of this system reveals that the ionic interactions can be tuned to create a broad range of morphologies, from microphases to macrophases.","author_count":8,"author_ids":["A5001305555","A5034985589","A5035039603","A5036216864","A5056892634","A5057560264","A5066596999","A5067839341"],"author_institution_ids":["I154570441"],"authors":[{"id":"A5066596999","name":"Shuyi Xie"},{"id":"A5057560264","name":"Kseniia M. Karnaukh"},{"id":"A5035039603","name":"Kai-Chieh Yang"},{"id":"A5056892634","name":"Dan Sun"},{"id":"A5034985589","name":"Kris T. Delaney"},{"id":"A5036216864","name":"Javier Read de Alaniz"},{"id":"A5067839341","name":"Glenn H. Fredrickson"},{"id":"A5001305555","name":"Rachel A. Segalman"}],"ca_any_author":true,"ca_led":true,"cited_by_count":32,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.macromol.3c00060","domain_tags":["analytical_methods"],"facet_tags":[],"institution_ids":["I154570441"],"is_oa":false,"journal":"Macromolecules","last_updated":"2026-11-10T11:39:43.021295","license":"","needs_review":false,"oa_status":"closed","oa_url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.macromol.3c00060","openalex_topics":["T10389","T10405","T11471"],"plastic_related":true,"plastic_tier":"core","primary_topic":"T10389","pub_date":"2023-05-08","study_type":"article","title":"Compatibilization of Polymer Blends by Ionic Bonding","type":"article","work_id":"W4375862850","year":2023}]}
